      title: "Ruth, Esther, and the Song of Songs",
      body: `## The five scrolls

Among the Writings are five short books, the **Megillot** ("scrolls"), each of which is read in the synagogue at a particular festival: the **Song of Songs** at Passover, **Ruth** at Shavuot (Weeks), **Lamentations** on the Ninth of Av (the anniversary of the destruction of the Temples), **Ecclesiastes** at Sukkot (Booths), and **Esther** at Purim. You have already met Lamentations and Ecclesiastes. Here we read the other three.

## Ruth

The book of **Ruth**, which you met briefly in lesson 3, is a short story of great beauty. In a time of famine, an Israelite family from Bethlehem, Elimelech, his wife **Naomi**, and their two sons, emigrate to **Moab**, where the sons marry Moabite women, **Orpah** and **Ruth**. Then all three men die. Naomi decides to return to Bethlehem, and urges her daughters-in-law to go back to their own mothers' houses. Orpah does so, but Ruth refuses:

> "Intreat me not to leave thee, or to return from following after thee: for whither thou goest, I will go; and where thou lodgest, I will lodge: thy people shall be my people, and thy God my God: Where thou diest, will I die, and there will I be buried." (Ruth 1:16–17)

In Bethlehem, Ruth goes to glean barley in the fields, following the reapers, as the law of Leviticus allowed the poor and the stranger to do, and happens to come to the field of **Boaz**, a wealthy relative of Elimelech. Boaz treats her kindly, having heard of her loyalty to Naomi. At Naomi's suggestion, Ruth goes to Boaz at night on the threshing floor, lies down at his feet, and asks him to "spread therefore thy skirt over thine handmaid; for thou art a near kinsman" (3:9), a request for marriage. Boaz, acting as **redeemer** (*go'el*), redeems the family's land and marries Ruth, and their son **Obed** becomes the grandfather of **David** (4:13–22).

The book's key word is ***hesed***, loyal love, which Ruth shows to Naomi, Boaz shows to Ruth, and God shows to all of them. Its heroine is a **foreigner**, a Moabite, a member of a people the law excluded, who becomes the ancestor of Israel's greatest king. Many scholars think the book was written, or at least read, as a quiet answer to the policies of Ezra and Nehemiah against foreign wives.

## Esther

The book of **Esther** is set in the Persian court at **Susa**, in the reign of **Ahasuerus** (probably Xerxes I, 486–465 BCE). When Queen Vashti refuses to display herself at the king's banquet, she is deposed, and a beautiful young Jewish woman, **Esther**, the cousin and ward of **Mordecai**, is chosen as the new queen, without revealing her Jewish identity. Mordecai refuses to bow down to the king's chief minister, **Haman**, who, enraged, persuades the king to issue a decree that all the Jews in the empire are to be killed on a single day, chosen by lot (*pur*). Mordecai urges Esther to intercede with the king, though to approach him unbidden is to risk death:

> "Think not with thyself that thou shalt escape in the king's house, more than all the Jews. For if thou altogether holdest thy peace at this time, then shall there enlargement and deliverance arise to the Jews from another place; … and who knoweth whether thou art come to the kingdom for such a time as this?" (Esther 4:13–14)

Esther answers: "I will go in unto the king, which is not according to the law: and if I perish, I perish" (4:16). She succeeds; Haman is hanged on the gallows he built for Mordecai; and the Jews are permitted to defend themselves against their enemies. The festival of **Purim** ("lots") celebrates the deliverance, with the reading of the book, feasting, gifts, and noisemakers to drown out Haman's name.

The book has a remarkable feature: in its Hebrew form, it **never mentions God**. There is no prayer, no miracle, no reference to the covenant or the law. God's hand, if present, is hidden behind chance, timing, and human courage, as in the story of Joseph. The Greek version adds prayers and references to God, which form the "Additions to Esther" in the Catholic and Orthodox Bibles. Esther was the only book of the Hebrew Bible not found among the Dead Sea Scrolls, and its place in the canon was debated. Most historians regard the story as a historical novella, not a record of actual events, though its picture of the Persian court contains authentic details.

## The Song of Songs

The **Song of Songs** (or Song of Solomon) is a collection of **love poems**, celebrating the passionate love of a woman and a man, in a lush landscape of gardens, vineyards, and spices:

> "Let him kiss me with the kisses of his mouth: for thy love is better than wine." (Song of Songs 1:2)

> "I am the rose of Sharon, and the lily of the valleys." (2:1)

> "Rise up, my love, my fair one, and come away. For, lo, the winter is past, the rain is over and gone; The flowers appear on the earth; the time of the singing of birds is come, and the voice of the turtle is heard in our land." (2:10–12)

> "Set me as a seal upon thine heart, as a seal upon thine arm: for love is strong as death; … Many waters cannot quench love, neither can the floods drown it." (8:6–7)

The Song never mentions God (except, possibly, in a word in 8:6), the covenant, or the law. The woman speaks more than the man, and expresses her desire freely. Many scholars compare it to Egyptian love poetry of the New Kingdom.

Why is it in the Bible? Its place in the canon was debated; but Rabbi **Akiva**, in the second century CE, declared: "All the Writings are holy, but the Song of Songs is the **holy of holies**" (Mishnah, *Yadayim* 3:5). Jewish tradition read it as an **allegory** of the love between God and Israel, and Christian tradition, from **Origen** in the third century to **Bernard of Clairvaux**, who preached eighty-six sermons on its first two chapters in the twelfth century, as an allegory of the love between Christ and the Church, or between God and the soul. Modern readers usually take it first as what it appears to be: a celebration of human love and desire as good, part of God's good creation, a return, some have suggested, to the garden of Eden, where the man and woman were naked and not ashamed.

> **Pause and consider.** The Hebrew book of Esther never mentions God, and the Song of Songs is a collection of love poems. Why might a community have included such books in its Scriptures? What might they reveal about how God is present in human life?`,
