      title: "Daniel, the Maccabees, and the Closing of the Canon",
      body: `## Daniel

The book of **Daniel** has two parts. Chapters 1–6 are **stories** about Daniel and his three friends, young Jewish exiles at the court of Babylon and Persia, who remain faithful to their God despite pressure and persecution: they refuse the king's food (Daniel 1); Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego refuse to worship Nebuchadnezzar's golden image, and are thrown into a **fiery furnace**, from which they emerge unharmed, with a fourth figure walking with them "like the Son of God" (Daniel 3:25); Daniel interprets the **writing on the wall** at the feast of Belshazzar, "MENE, MENE, TEKEL, UPHARSIN," announcing the fall of Babylon (Daniel 5); and Daniel, for praying to his God against the king's decree, is thrown into the **lions' den**, and God shuts the lions' mouths (Daniel 6).

Chapters 7–12 are **visions**, in which Daniel sees the future history of the world as a series of beasts rising from the sea, representing four empires, followed by the judgment of God:

> "I beheld till the thrones were cast down, and the Ancient of days did sit, whose garment was white as snow, and the hair of his head like the pure wool. … I saw in the night visions, and, behold, one like **the Son of man** came with the clouds of heaven, and came to the Ancient of days, … And there was given him dominion, and glory, and a kingdom, that all people, nations, and languages, should serve him: his dominion is an everlasting dominion." (Daniel 7:9, 13–14)

The figure "like a son of man," a human figure in contrast to the beasts, may originally have represented the faithful people of Israel, "the saints of the most High" (7:18), receiving the kingdom. In later Jewish and Christian tradition, he was understood as a heavenly **Messiah**; in the Gospels, "the Son of man" is Jesus's most common way of referring to himself.

## Antiochus and the Maccabees

The visions of Daniel point to a specific historical crisis. After Alexander the Great's conquest of the Persian Empire in 332 BCE, Judea came under the rule of his successors, first the Ptolemies of Egypt, then the **Seleucids** of Syria. In **167 BCE**, the Seleucid king **Antiochus IV Epiphanes** outlawed the practice of Judaism, forbidding circumcision, Sabbath observance, and the reading of the Torah, and set up an altar to Zeus in the Temple in Jerusalem, "the **abomination of desolation**" (Daniel 11:31; 1 Maccabees 1:54). A priest named **Mattathias** and his sons, led by **Judas Maccabeus** ("the Hammer"), rose in revolt, and in **164 BCE** recaptured Jerusalem and **rededicated** the Temple, an event celebrated in the festival of **Hanukkah** ("dedication"). The story is told in the deuterocanonical books of **1 and 2 Maccabees**.

Most critical scholars believe that the book of Daniel, though set in the sixth century BCE, was completed during the persecution of Antiochus, around **165 BCE**. Its visions describe the history of the Persian and Hellenistic empires accurately up to the persecution, and then become inaccurate in predicting the details of Antiochus's death, which suggests that it was written just before. Its purpose was to encourage the faithful under persecution, by assuring them that God controls history, and that the persecutor's power is about to end. Traditional interpreters, Jewish and Christian, have defended the sixth-century date.

## Apocalyptic and resurrection

Daniel is the only full example in the Hebrew Bible of **apocalyptic** literature (from the Greek *apokalypsis*, "revelation" or "unveiling"), a genre that flourished in Judaism from the third century BCE to the second century CE, and of which the New Testament book of **Revelation** is the most famous Christian example. Apocalypses reveal, through visions interpreted by angels, the hidden meaning of history and the coming end of the present age, when God will judge the world and establish his kingdom.

Daniel also contains the clearest statement in the Hebrew Bible of belief in the **resurrection** of the dead and judgment after death:

> "And many of them that sleep in the dust of the earth shall awake, some to everlasting life, and some to shame and everlasting contempt. And they that be wise shall shine as the brightness of the firmament; and they that turn many to righteousness as the stars for ever and ever." (Daniel 12:2–3)

For most of the Hebrew Bible, the dead go down to **Sheol**, a shadowy underworld, where "the dead praise not the LORD, neither any that go down into silence" (Psalm 115:17). The hope of resurrection emerged, it seems, in the time of the martyrs, when the faithful were dying for their faith, and justice seemed to require that God would raise them. It became a central belief of the Pharisees, of rabbinic Judaism, and of Christianity.

## The closing of the canon

How did these books become the **canon**, the authoritative list of Scripture? The process was gradual, and the details are uncertain. The Torah seems to have been authoritative by the fifth or fourth century BCE, and the Prophets by the second. The boundaries of the Writings remained open longer: the Dead Sea Scrolls community seems to have regarded other books, such as Jubilees and 1 Enoch, as authoritative, and the Septuagint included books not in the later Hebrew canon. By the end of the first century CE, the Jewish historian **Josephus** spoke of twenty-two books, "justly believed to be divine" (*Against Apion* 1.38–41), and the apocalypse 4 Ezra of twenty-four (14:44–46), which are probably the books of the Tanakh, counted differently.

An older theory held that the canon was officially closed by a "**Council of Jamnia**" (Yavneh), a gathering of rabbis around 90 CE, after the destruction of the Temple. Most scholars now regard this as a myth: there was no council in the sense of a church council, and the rabbinic discussions at Yavneh concerned only whether certain books, such as Ecclesiastes and the Song of Songs, "defile the hands," that is, were holy, and did not create a canon. The canon seems to have emerged by consensus.

Christians, using the Greek Bible, kept a wider collection. The debate over the deuterocanonical books continued in the Church: **Jerome**, who translated the Bible into Latin around 400 CE, preferred the Hebrew canon, while **Augustine** accepted the wider one. As you saw in lesson 1, the issue was settled differently by Protestants and Catholics in the sixteenth century.

## The legacy

The Hebrew Bible became the foundation of two religions, Judaism and Christianity, and deeply influenced a third, Islam, whose Qur'an tells of Adam, Noah, Abraham, Moses, David, Solomon, and Jonah. It shaped Western law, with the idea that rulers are subject to a higher law; politics, through the stories of the Exodus and the prophets' demand for justice; literature, from Milton's *Paradise Lost* to Toni Morrison's *Song of Solomon*; art and music, from Michelangelo to Handel; and the moral imagination of billions of people. Its ideas, that human beings are made in the image of God, that the stranger and the poor must be protected, that the powerful must answer for their deeds, that history has a direction and a purpose, that there is one God of all the earth, have become so familiar that we forget how revolutionary they once were.

> **Pause and consider.** Daniel's visions encouraged people facing persecution by assuring them that God controls history and that the persecutor's power would end. Why might apocalyptic hope be especially powerful in times of oppression? What are its dangers?`,
